[{"learnocaml_version":"2","kind":"exercise","title":"Geometry","short_description":"A less simple pattern-matching exercise","stars":1,"identifier":"geometry","authors":[["Max Kopinsky","max.kopinsky@mcgill.ca"],["Jacob Errington","jacob.errington@mcgill.ca"]]},{"id":"geometry","prelude":"let pi = 3.14 (* close enough, right? *)\n\ntype shape = Square of float | Circle of float | Rectangle of float * float\n","template":"let perimeter (shape : shape) : float = failwith \"TODO\"\n\nlet area (shape : shape) : float = failwith \"TODO\"\n","descr":[["","<h1 id=\"traffic-safety\">Geometry</h1>\n<p>This problem is a simple exercise with a datatype having some fields and\npattern matching.</p>\n<p>You are given the definition\n<code>type shape = Square of float | Circle of float | Rectangle of int * int</code>.\nYou can see this definition in a new pane above the editor to the left. This pane is\ncalled the <em>prelude</em>. You can use anything defined there, but you\ncan't edit it. You <strong>must not</strong> copy the prelude into your\nown code - this would make your code <em>shadow</em> the prelude, and\nthen your code will no longer be compatible with the grader.</p>\n\n<p>We want to calculate the perimeter and area of these shapes.\nImplement the functions\n<code>perimeter : shape -&gt; float</code>\nand\n<code>area : shape -&gt; float</code>\neach of which takes\na shape as input.\nYou must use <code>match</code> (or <code>function</code>)\nand must not use <code>if</code>.</p>\nTo calculate the perimeter and area of the circle, use the provided constant <code>pi</code> from the prelude.\n"]],"prepare":"dydouPNdgkfH6ug4TjIbyiVKXkt+C5tS359TPmx=","test":"","solution":"","max-score":24,"depend":null,"dependencies":[]},null]